 1| //! The admin API: contributor trust, index status, knowledge documents.
 2| //!
 3| //! Feature-gated behind `serve` along with the rest of `src/server`.
 4| //!
 5| //! These routes mutate operator state, so they are authenticated the same way
 6| //! the webhook is and for the same reason: anyone on the internet can reach
 7| //! them. The webhook has GitHub's HMAC; there is no equivalent for a human with
 8| //! `curl`, so the door is a bearer token compared in constant time, checked in
 9| //! a `route_layer` — that is, **before** any handler extractor parses a body.
10| //!
11| //! The token is not optional and there is no default. When
12| //! `TINYSWEEPER_ADMIN_TOKEN` is unset the admin router is never mounted at all
13| //! and every path under `/admin` 404s, because the alternative to fail-closed
14| //! here is shipping an unauthenticated write endpoint.
15| //!
16| //! Every route is implemented. The index routes answer `503` on a deployment
17| //! with no embedding provider, which is not a stub: there is genuinely no index
18| //! to report on, and saying so is a different answer from an empty one.
19| //!
20| //! The knowledge routes are the write side of the knowledge centre. A document
21| //! body reaches a review prompt, so these endpoints are the *only* trusted way
22| //! into it: a repository's own `AGENTS.md` gets the sandboxed extraction pass
23| //! in `crate::knowledge::extract` instead, precisely because nobody
